.TH TRESTLE 8 "October 2026" "trestle 0.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
trestle \- CNI and Netavark plugin that attaches (rootless) containers to host bridges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trestle info
.br
.B trestle create
< \fInetwork.json\fR
.br
.B trestle setup
\fInetns-path\fR < \fIplugin-exec.json\fR
.br
.B trestle teardown
\fInetns-path\fR < \fIplugin-exec.json\fR
.br
.BR CNI_COMMAND = ADD | DEL | CHECK | VERSION
.B trestle
< \fInetwork-config.json\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trestle
is the unprivileged half of the trestle network plugin. Container runtimes
execute it either as a Netavark plugin (Podman, sub commands as arguments)
or as a CNI plugin (Kubernetes / containerd / CRI-O, \fBCNI_*\fR environment
variables). It never touches the network itself: it validates the call,
reads the JSON document from standard input and forwards everything to
.BR trestled (8)
over the unix socket configured in
.BR trestle.conf (5)
(default \fI/tmp/trestle.sock\fR). The exact output of the daemon \- a CNI
result or a Netavark status block \- is printed on standard output and its
exit status is returned.
.PP
The binary is installed as \fI/usr/libexec/trestle/trestle\fR with symbolic
links in the Netavark plugin directory and the CNI binary directory, so the
network driver / plugin type is called \fBtrestle\fR.
.SS Rootless containers
When the client runs as an unprivileged user or as uid 0 inside a user
namespace (rootless Podman runs Netavark like that), the network namespace
path given to \fBsetup\fR only exists in the caller's private mount
namespace. The client opens it and passes the open file descriptor to the
daemon with \fBSCM_RIGHTS\fR. The daemon only accepts a namespace that is
owned by a user namespace created by the calling user.
.SS Local answers
\fBtrestle info\fR and \fBCNI_COMMAND=VERSION\fR are answered by the client
itself, so they work while the daemon is stopped.
.SS Verifying the daemon
Before sending anything the client checks that the socket is served by a root
process running the configured \fBserver_binary\fR (see \fBverify_server\fR in
.BR trestle.conf (5)).
Inside a rootless user namespace the kernel does not allow reading
\fI/proc/<pid>/exe\fR of a root process; in the default \fBrelaxed\fR mode the
client then checks the process name and that the peer and the socket file are
owned by root (seen as the overflow uid).
.SH NETAVARK
.TP
.B create
Called by \fBpodman network create \-d trestle\fR. The network must be
configured in trestle and the user permitted. The bridge is created and the
returned configuration carries the bridge name, the subnet (when
\fBpodman_ipam=host-local\fR) and the IPAM driver.
.TP
.BI setup " netns"
Creates the veth pair, assigns the address and default route and prints the
status block.
.TP
.BI teardown " netns"
Removes the host side of the veth (which also removes the peer), releases the
address and removes DNS / hosts entries.
.SH CNI
Supported versions: 0.3.0, 0.3.1, 0.4.0 and 1.0.0. Commands: ADD, DEL, CHECK
and VERSION. The container name used for naming is
\fIK8S_POD_NAMESPACE\fR\-\fIK8S_POD_NAME\fR from \fBCNI_ARGS\fR, or the
first 12 characters of \fBCNI_CONTAINERID\fR. Static addresses can be
requested with \fBIP=\fR in \fBCNI_ARGS\fR or the \fBips\fR capability
(\fBruntimeConfig.ips\fR), MAC addresses with \fBMAC=\fR or
\fBruntimeConfig.mac\fR.
.PP
Example network configuration:
.PP
.nf
.RS
{ "cniVersion": "1.0.0", "name": "lab", "type": "trestle" }
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRESTLE_CONFIG
Alternative configuration file (only \fBsocket\fR, \fBsocket_owner\fR,
\fBserver_binary\fR, \fBverify_server\fR, \fBserver_selinux_type\fR and
\fBrequest_timeout\fR matter to the client).
.TP
.B TRESTLE_DEBUG
Print debug messages on standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, otherwise 1 with an error document on standard output
(\fB{"error": ...}\fR for Netavark, a CNI error object for CNI).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/trestle/trestle.conf
.TP
.I /tmp/trestle.sock
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trestled (8),
.BR trestle.conf (5),
.BR podman-network-create (1),
.BR netavark (1)
